public_question.prompt:
Fictitious Bins(id) contains 61, 62, 63. Tags(bin_id, color) contains (61, 'red') and (62, 'blue'). Complete the result count with one digit.
SELECT b.id FROM Bins b LEFT JOIN Tags t ON b.id = t.bin_id WHERE t.color = 'red';
Number of result rows: ____

Fictitious Routes(id) contains 71, 72, 73. Departures(route_id, delayed) contains (71, 1) and (72, 0).
A: SELECT r.id, d.delayed FROM Routes r LEFT JOIN Departures d ON r.id = d.route_id AND d.delayed = 1;
B: SELECT r.id, d.delayed FROM Routes r LEFT JOIN Departures d ON r.id = d.route_id WHERE d.delayed = 1;
Give each query's result rows, ignoring order. Explain the difference for routes 72 and 73, then choose a query for a report that must include every route while attaching only delayed departures.

Fictitious Gardens contains G1 and G2. Visits contains (V1, G1, planned); G2 has no visit. This query is intended to show every garden and only completed visits:
SELECT g.id, v.id FROM Gardens g LEFT JOIN Visits v ON v.garden_id = g.id WHERE v.state = 'completed';
Explain why neither garden appears. Rewrite the query to meet the intention, and state the rewritten result rows.

Fictitious Rooms contains IDs 91, 92, 93. Reservations(id, room_id, confirmed) contains (1201, 91, 1) and (1202, 92, 0).
Query X: SELECT r.id, v.id FROM Rooms r LEFT JOIN Reservations v ON r.id = v.room_id AND v.confirmed = 1;
Query Y: SELECT r.id, v.id FROM Rooms r LEFT JOIN Reservations v ON r.id = v.room_id WHERE v.confirmed = 1;
Give the result rows for each query, ignoring order. Explain why rooms 92 and 93 behave as they do, and identify which query meets the request “show every room with any confirmed reservations.”

Fictitious Members contains IDs 1 and 2. Notes(id, member_id, text) contains (50, 1, NULL); note IDs are never NULL. Consider:
SELECT m.id, n.id, n.text FROM Members m LEFT JOIN Notes n ON m.id = n.member_id WHERE n.text IS NULL;
Which interpretation is correct?

Fictitious Studios contains one row, id 8. Bookings contains no rows. Complete the numeric result:
SELECT COUNT(b.id) FROM Studios s LEFT JOIN Bookings b ON s.id = b.studio_id;
The query returns ____.

In a fictitious database, Devices contains IDs 4 and 5. Warranties(id, device_id, comment) contains (90, 4, NULL); warranty IDs are never NULL. A learner claims this query lists only devices without a warranty:
SELECT d.id FROM Devices d LEFT JOIN Warranties w ON d.id = w.device_id WHERE w.comment IS NULL;
State which IDs it actually returns, explain the error, and replace only the WHERE condition to meet the claimed goal.

A learner claims: “If every left row has at least one right row with the same ID, moving a right-side filter from ON to WHERE cannot change a LEFT JOIN result.” Disprove the claim using the smallest possible fictitious dataset for L(id) and R(id, flag), with non-NULL IDs and flag values 0 or 1.
Compare:
A: SELECT l.id, r.flag FROM L l LEFT JOIN R r ON l.id = r.id AND r.flag = 1;
B: SELECT l.id, r.flag FROM L l LEFT JOIN R r ON l.id = r.id WHERE r.flag = 1;
Give your dataset, each result, and the reason they differ.
